Logical replication by tuple identifier (REPLICA IDENTITY ROWID)
================================================================

Purpose
-------

Tables without a primary key can only replicate UPDATE and DELETE with
REPLICA IDENTITY FULL, which logs the whole old row and makes the subscriber
search for a matching row.  REPLICA IDENTITY ROWID instead identifies rows by
their tuple identifier (TID, the ctid system column).  The publisher logs no
old column values at all: the WAL record of every heap INSERT, UPDATE and
DELETE already contains the block number and line pointer offset of the row
versions involved, because redo needs them.  The intended use is migrating
such tables, typically to a newer major version.

Overview
--------

Publisher:

  decode.c        records the TID of the new row version in newtuple->t_self
                  and of the old row version in ReorderBufferChange.old_tid
                  (always; the latter even if no old tuple data is logged).
  reorderbuffer.c passes them to output plugins only for ROWID tables, or if
                  logical_decoding_expose_headers is on; otherwise they are
                  invalidated, as output plugins always saw them before.
  proto.c,        send the TID as an additional virtual column ".rowid" of
  pgoutput.c      type tid, marked as replica identity.  UPDATE always sends
                  a key tuple ('K') with just the old TID; DELETE likewise.
  tablecmds.c,    refuse to rewrite ROWID tables while logical decoding is
  cluster.c,      possible (CheckRelationRewriteAllowed()).
  vacuum.c

Subscriber:

  tablesync.c     fetches the TID as ctid with COPY (SELECT ..., ctid ...),
                  and refuses to synchronize into a non-empty table.
  execMain.c      refuses UPDATE/DELETE/MERGE/ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE on
                  "ROWID targets" (RelationIsRowidTarget(): the replica
                  identity index is the single column ".rowid" tid), except
                  by logical replication workers or with
                  session_replication_role = replica.

Applying changes needs no new code: the apply worker finds rows using the
replica identity index on ".rowid", like for any other key.

Why a virtual column
--------------------

Modelling the TID as an ordinary key column means nothing is new as far as
the protocol is concerned.  Any subscriber, including older releases, can
apply the changes to a table with a column ".rowid" tid that has a primary
key or replica identity index on it.  Newer subscribers add correct initial
synchronization and protection of the target table.

Correctness of the TIDs
-----------------------

The TIDs come only from the WAL record of the committed operation: the same
block reference and offset number that redo uses.  They never come from a
column value; a ROWID table cannot have a column named ".rowid".  Aborted
transactions and subtransactions are never decoded (in streaming mode their
changes are discarded by the subscriber on STREAM ABORT).

A live row keeps its TID until it is updated or deleted, and both are
replicated (UPDATE as old TID -> new TID).  HOT pruning turns the root line
pointer of a HOT chain into a redirect but never moves the line pointer of a
live tuple; page defragmentation moves tuple data but keeps offsets.  A TID
becomes reusable only after the DELETE (or UPDATE) that freed it has
committed and its tuple has been pruned; as decoding follows commit order,
the subscriber applies that DELETE before any INSERT reusing the TID.

Only rewriting the table (VACUUM FULL, CLUSTER, ALTER TABLE forms that
rewrite) assigns new TIDs without decoded changes, so it is refused.
TRUNCATE is replicated, so it is safe.

The initial COPY runs with the snapshot exported when the replication slot
was created, so the copied TIDs agree with the changes that follow.

Restrictions and future work
----------------------------

- Partitioned tables: TIDs are only unique within a partition.  Partitions
  can use ROWID but cannot be published via the partition root.  A key of
  (tableoid, ctid) would lift this.
- Row filters cannot be evaluated for UPDATE and DELETE without the old row.
  This could be lifted by logging the columns the filters need.
- CLUSTER and VACUUM FULL could be allowed
  if they emitted a logical message mapping old to new TIDs, which
  subscribers would apply to ".rowid".
